.TH TUI_FILECHOOSER_OPEN 3 2026-11-15 "libtuikit 1.2" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_filechooser_open, tui_filechooser_set_hidden, tui_filechooser_loading,
tui_filechooser_poll, tui_filechooser_dir, tui_filechooser_handle,
tui_filechooser_render, tui_filechooser_free \-
a modal dialog to open a file, choose a directory or name a file to save
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/filechooser.h>
.PP
.BI "int tui_filechooser_open(tui_filechooser *" fc ", const char *" title ", tui_fc_mode " mode ,
.BI "                         const char *" start ", const char *" filter );
.BI "void tui_filechooser_set_hidden(tui_filechooser *" fc ", int " show );
.BI "int tui_filechooser_loading(const tui_filechooser *" fc );
.BI "int tui_filechooser_poll(tui_filechooser *" fc );
.BI "const char *tui_filechooser_dir(const tui_filechooser *" fc );
.BI "tui_fc_state tui_filechooser_handle(tui_filechooser *" fc ", const tui_event *" ev );
.BI "void tui_filechooser_render(tui_filechooser *" fc ", tui_canvas " c );
.BI "void tui_filechooser_free(tui_filechooser *" fc );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tui_filechooser
is the file browser every program asks the library for.
It is a modal dialog in the style of
.BR tui_dialog :
it does not block and does not read the terminal.
The program routes events to it first while it is open, draws it last, and
reads the outcome once
.I state
is no longer
.BR TUI_FC_ACTIVE .
.PP
The dialog has a
.B typing field
with the path, labelled \(lqOpen:\(rq, \(lqDirectory:\(rq or \(lqSave as:\(rq, under it a rule
with the status set in it, and the
.B list
of the entries of the directory: directories first, then files, each group
by name without regard to case, with the size of a file and the date of
change.
A link to a directory is a directory.
The list takes the keys of the list widgets: Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home and
End (a page is the rows less two, at least one; the letters j, k, g and G are typed into the field).
.PP
.B The field and the list follow each other.
A key that moves the list puts the path of the row into the field (a
directory with a trailing slash); in \fBTUI_FC_SAVE\fR it leaves the field alone, so the file
name stays, and Enter on a directory row enters it with the name kept (only the directory
part of the field changes); Enter on a file row chooses that file (after the replace question).
What is typed selects the first entry whose name starts with it, and a path
with a slash in it shows that directory.
Tab completes the name being typed from the entries shown: all of it when
one matches (a directory with a slash), the common beginning when several do.
A leading \fB~\fR, \fB~/\fR or \fB~user/\fR in the field is looked up as that home directory
($HOME for the caller's own), like an absolute path; the field keeps the text as typed.
A \fB~\fR with no home is taken as typed.
Names with spaces need no quoting.
The field is measured in display cells: it scrolls to keep the cursor in view, and the
cursor stands after accented and wide characters at their cell column.
.PP
.B Enter
acts on the path in the field: a directory is entered, a file is chosen
(\fBTUI_FC_OPEN\fR), a name that does not exist is refused with a line in the
status row (a new name is accepted in \fBTUI_FC_SAVE\fR).
.B Backspace
with nothing typed after the directory goes up one directory and stands on
the one it came out of; the row
.B ../
goes up on Enter.
.B Alt-H
shows or hides the names that start with a dot.
.B Esc
and Ctrl-C cancel.
.PP
The modes:
.TP
.B TUI_FC_OPEN
an existing file.
.TP
.B TUI_FC_DIR
a directory; only directories are listed, and the first row is
.B ./
(the directory shown).
Enter on a directory goes into it; Enter on the field when it shows the
directory itself (the row
.B ./
selected, or the path ended with a slash) chooses it.
.TP
.B TUI_FC_SAVE
the name of a file to write: the name is typed, the directory is navigated.
A name that exists asks
.I Replace it?
first; only Yes (or y) chooses it.
.PP
.BR tui_filechooser_open ()
starts the dialog in
.I start
(NULL: the current directory; a relative path is taken from it), with the
frame title
.I title
(NULL: \(lqOpen file\(rq, \(lqChoose directory\(rq or \(lqSave as\(rq) and
only the files whose names match the shell pattern
.I filter
(\fBfnmatch\fR(3); NULL: all), directories always.
It returns at once: the directory is read by a background thread, so a slow
mount does not stop the keys; the typing field works meanwhile and the status
row says \(lqReading...\(rq.
.I notify_fd
(a descriptor of the dialog) becomes readable when a read has ended: a program
that waits with
.BR tui_term_wait (3)
gives it as an extra descriptor and calls
.BR tui_filechooser_poll ()
when it is ready.
.BR tui_filechooser_handle ()
and
.BR tui_filechooser_render ()
take in a finished read themselves, so a program that does not wait on the
descriptor sees the list with the next key or redraw.
.BR tui_filechooser_poll ()
returns 1 when the list changed.
.BR tui_filechooser_loading ()
is 1 while a read is on.
.PP
.BR tui_filechooser_dir ()
is the directory shown, absolute with a trailing slash.
.BR tui_filechooser_set_hidden ()
sets what Alt-H toggles (default: dot names hidden).
.BR tui_filechooser_handle ()
gives the dialog one event and returns its state;
.B TUI_FC_OK
means that
.I path
holds the chosen absolute path, normalised (no
.B .
or
.B ..
or doubled slash, no trailing slash except for
.BR / ),
valid until
.BR tui_filechooser_free ().
.BR tui_filechooser_render ()
draws the dialog centred in the canvas, sized to at most 76 by 20 cells, and
puts the terminal cursor in the typing field.
.BR tui_filechooser_free ()
frees everything; a read still running is left to end by itself.
It is safe after a failed open and on an already freed dialog.
.PP
The look is the scheme's: the frame in the
.B frame
role, the field in
.BR input.focus ,
the selected row in the table's selection (a dark blue ground and a
\(lq>\(rq in the first column), the status row in
.BR hint ,
.B progress
or
.BR error .
A narrow dialog drops the size and the date.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.BR tui_filechooser_open ()
returns 0, or \-1 with
.B errno
set:
.B EINVAL
(a NULL dialog, a mode out of range),
.B ENOMEM
or what
.BR socketpair (2)
reports.
A directory that cannot be read is not an error of the call: the status row
says \(lqcannot read:\(rq and why.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libtuikit (7),
.BR tui_dialog_message (3),
.BR tui_term_wait (3),
.BR tui_lineedit_init (3)
